  "slug": "big-brains-00f8",
  "summary": "Neuro-evolution in a seasonal foraging task contrasts large brains evolving in changing environments with large brains arising from earlier static ones.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across the two more closely related preprints is causal attribution in adaptive systems: whether an observed trait reflects the environment that selected it or a prior condition that shaped it. The brain-size preprint uses neuro-evolution in an artificial seasonal foraging task to contrast large brains evolving in changing environments with large brains arising from earlier static ones. The gut-brain agent-based model shows that sensory traits shape learned diet and microbiome, and that microbiome similarity alone does not uniquely identify the cause, framing the outcome as behavioural degeneracy. The neural-astrocyte paper is less directly aligned, modelling astrocytes as enabling evidence accumulation and context switching in reinforcement learning via a hybrid automaton. What is new in the first two preprints is explicit modelling of alternative pathways to comparable outcomes rather than assumption of a single driver.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-09-15"
}
